# Set up the project

This walkthrough uses Node and TypeScript to expose one RPC, call it through
Trellis, and shut down cleanly. It returns an order receipt without persisting
orders. No database, ORM, or configuration library is involved. These are
example choices, not requirements for a Trellis service.

For Rust connection and provider APIs, see the
[Rust library guide](/guides/libraries/rust). The native contract and generation
workflow are shared by both languages.

## Prerequisites

- Install [the Trellis CLI](/guides/install-trellis-cli) matching your
  libraries.
- Install Node 24 and npm 11 for this example.
- For manual deployment, have a [running Trellis](/guides/starting-trellis) and
  an administrator login. The [automated test](/guides/testing-trellis-services)
  instead starts an isolated runtime using `trellis-server`.

These pages track the repository source. If using an older released CLI, use the
documentation from that release rather than combining authoring generations.

## Create a standalone project

```sh
mkdir orders-service
cd orders-service
npm init -y
npm install @oatscenter/trellis ulid
npm install --save-dev @oatscenter/trellis-testkit @types/node typescript
npm pkg set type=module 'scripts.check=tsc --noEmit' 'scripts.test=node --test service_test.ts' 'scripts.start=node main.ts'
```

Select matching released versions when installing packages. Keep the import
package dependencies that npm writes; generated code uses those package specifiers.
The test package is only needed to run the live test.

Create `trellis.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "orders"
version = "1.0.0"

[sources]
orders = "contract.trellis"

[generate.typescript]
output = "trellis"
```

The tutorial defines its provider and caller API in the same project, so no
external API dependency is needed. `trellis.toml` is a Trellis compiler
manifest, not the service's runtime configuration file.

Import the generated ESM entrypoint directly from `./trellis/index.js`.
The generator never edits your npm configuration.

Create `tsconfig.json` for the ordinary TypeScript check:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ESNext", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true
  },
  "include": ["*.ts"]
}
```

The resulting authored project will contain:

```text
orders-service/
  trellis.toml
  trellis.lock
  contract.trellis
  package.json
  package-lock.json
  tsconfig.json
  service.ts
  main.ts
  service_test.ts
  trellis/          # one ignored, generated package
```

## Run the checked-in example instead

The same source is checked in under
[`docs/examples/orders`](https://github.com/oats-center/trellis/tree/main/docs/examples/orders).
Its generated package is disposable. Generate it from the committed native IDL
before checking or testing. Its tasks use ordinary installed libraries, not
parent-repository paths:

```sh
cd docs/examples/orders
npm install
trellis install
npm run check
npm test
```

The live test still needs the matching `trellis-server` runtime executable and
pinned NATS binaries. Continue with
[the contract](/guides/write-a-service/first-contract).
